# Envelope kinematics from PV diagrams (infall, rotation, centrifugal barrier)

A PV diagram cut along the disk major axis (perpendicular to the outflow) and along the minor axis separates
rotation from infall: rotation gives antisymmetric emission in opposite quadrants along the major axis, infall
adds a velocity gradient along the minor axis and diamond or skewed shapes near the centre. Papers turn the PV
data into (a) a rotation profile V(R) fit with power laws, (b) a forward model of an infalling-rotating envelope
plus disk compared with the PV diagrams, or (c) infall evidence from absorption against the continuum. The
mistakes that matter most: (1) trusting low-velocity and large-radius PV points, where interferometric
filtering and self-absorption distort the profile (2014ApJ...796..131O kept only R<100 AU because about 50% of
~1000 AU-scale emission was resolved out), and (2) quoting masses, break radii and infall speeds without
saying how inclination, systemic velocity and resolution bias were handled (the mean-position method
"may systematically underestimate" the break radius at limited resolution, 2015ApJ...812...27A).

> Always report: line and its optical depth, PV cut PA and width, systemic velocity and whether it was fit,
> inclination and its source, distance, emission threshold for data points, velocity ranges excluded, and how
> models were spatially filtered. The pack's cards flag PV cut widths and extraction details as not stated in
> several envelope papers (2014ApJ...796..131O, 2017ApJ...834..178Y, 2020A&A...634L..12D).

**Scope of the evidence.** The regex tagger pulled 213 papers; the science-field aggregate lists star
formation 124, protoplanetary disks 47, nearby galaxies 37, AGN 29, evolved stars 21 (pack aggregates; papers
carry several fields). Many hits are outflow, galaxy or AGB-star PV work. This skill uses the roughly 50 papers
on protostellar envelopes, cores and streamers (my count) and cites the others only for generic PV practice.

## When to use

- A Class 0/I protostar shows a velocity gradient in C18O, 13CO, CS, SO, CH3OH or HCO+ and you need the
  transition from infall to Keplerian rotation (2014ApJ...796..131O, 2015ApJ...812...27A, 2017ApJ...849...56A,
  2017ApJ...834..178Y, 2018ApJ...864L..25O).
- You want the centrifugal barrier radius and protostellar mass from an infalling-rotating envelope model
  (2015ApJ...812...59O, 2016ApJ...824...88O, 2017ApJ...837..174O, 2019ApJ...873...73Z, 2024ApJ...970...28O).
- Pseudodisk or flattened envelope with infall plus angular-momentum-conserving rotation (2014ApJ...786..114L,
  2017ApJ...843...27L, 2019ApJ...879..101L, 2019ApJ...871..100H).
- Streamers feeding a disk: trajectory fits, infall time, mass infall rate (2024A&A...683A.133G,
  2025SciA...11.4512O, 2025ApJ...993..120H, 2019A&A...623A..96Y).
- Infall from redshifted (inverse P-Cygni) absorption against compact continuum (2015ApJ...814...22E,
  2012A&A...544L...7P, 2013ApJ...764L..14Z, 2018A&A...615A.141B, 2018ApJ...869..158F).
- Massive hot cores and disks with rotation plus infall (2025A&A...693A..76C, 2017A&A...603A..10B,
  2015ApJ...813L..19J, 2019ApJ...877L..25M, 2024ApJ...967...15K).

## When not to use

| Situation | Use instead |
|---|---|
| A settled, mostly Keplerian protoplanetary or Class II disk; you want M*, emission height, i, PA | `keplerian-disk-kinematics` skill (channel-map or cube fitting). Use this skill only for the envelope-to-disk transition |
| Rotation curves, dispersion and inflow/outflow in galaxies (3DBarolo, GalPak3D, kinemetry) | `galaxy-3d-kinematics` skill; `smbh-gas-dynamics` for black-hole masses. Galaxy PV papers in this pack (2021A&A...655A..25Z, 2020MNRAS.493.3744F, 2019A&A...623A..79C) follow that workflow |
| Outflow mass, momentum, energetics, rotating outflows or disk winds | `outflow-energetics` skill. Outflow PV papers here (2018A&A...618A.120L, 2020A&A...634L..12D) measure j in the flow, not the envelope |
| AGB or post-AGB expanding or rotating envelopes | `evolved-star-envelopes` skill (2018A&A...614A.113H, 2020ApJ...890L..14T are in this pack only by regex) |
| Cluster-scale or filament inflow without a central protostar's potential | Hub-filament velocity-gradient work (2024A&A...690A.185W uses region rest velocity as a proxy for core velocity); this skill's point-mass models do not apply |
| Single moment-1 map is all you need for PA | `moment-maps` skill |

## Steps

1. **Fix the geometry before cutting PV diagrams.**
   - Centre: continuum peak or a 2D Gaussian fit to it (2015ApJ...814...22E fit the continuum position with a
     2D Gaussian; 2024ApJ...963...72O took i=51.1 deg from the continuum).
   - PA of the disk/envelope major axis: perpendicular to the jet or outflow (2019ApJ...873...73Z cut at
     PA 20 deg perpendicular to the SiO jet), or from the line emission itself; 2018A&A...614A.113H made
     orthogonal PV pairs every 5 deg to find the PA (uncertainty ~5 deg).
   - Inclination: from continuum aspect ratio, i = arccos(minor/major) for a thin disk (2017ApJ...845...58Z,
     2018A&A...619A..39F), from an outflow model fit (2017ApJ...834..178Y grid in 5 deg steps, checked against
     ~50 deg from continuum), or fixed edge-on for L1527 (85 deg in 2017ApJ...849...56A, 90 deg in
     2014ApJ...796..131O).
   - Systemic velocity: fit it, then check. 2017ApJ...849...56A and 2017ApJ...834..178Y left Vsys free;
     2024A&A...690A..46S fixed it to 2.84 km/s after a first fit; 2018ApJ...864L..25O found 5.3 km/s fit better
     than 5.5 km/s.
2. **Choose lines by what they trace.** Optically thin isotopologues (C18O, 13CO) for the rotation profile
   (2017ApJ...849...56A, 2015ApJ...812...27A, 2017ApJ...834..178Y); CS, OCS, SO, CH3OH, H2CO, HCOOH to resolve
   the envelope and centrifugal barrier chemistry (2015ApJ...812...59O, 2016ApJ...824...88O,
   2014ApJ...796..131O, 2024ApJ...970...28O); 12CO only at high velocity (2019ApJ...871..100H fit C18O at
   |V-Vlsr|<1.6 km/s and CO above 1.6 km/s). Optically thick lines limit what you can infer
   (2014ApJ...796..131O: SO optically thick, so density and abundance unconstrained).
3. **Extract the PV diagrams.** Major and minor axis cuts through the centre are the standard pair
   (2015ApJ...812...27A, 2019ApJ...871..100H); 2015ApJ...812...59O compared six cuts. Cut width: one beam
   (2018ApJ...863...19A) or a few tenths of an arcsec (0.2 arcsec in 2019A&A...621A..83S, 0.5 arcsec in
   2018A&A...619A..39F). Tools: CASA `impv`, `pvextractor`, GILDAS (pack aggregates; Step 5 of
   2025SciA...11.4512O used pvextractor along a streamer spine).
4. **Measure the rotation profile V(R)** (the "ridge" approach).
   - Per channel, take the intensity-weighted mean position of pixels above 5-6 sigma (5 sigma in
     2017ApJ...849...56A, 6 sigma in 2015ApJ...812...27A) or a Gaussian-fit peak (2020ApJ...893...51S fit
     positions per channel for the inner part and velocities per position for the outer part).
   - Drop low velocities where filtering and envelope confusion dominate: |dV|<1.2 km/s in 2015ApJ...812...27A;
     low-velocity channels excluded in 2017ApJ...834..178Y.
   - Fit V = V_b (R/R_b)^-p in log R - log V, single and double power law, chi2 (2017ApJ...849...56A,
     2015ApJ...812...27A, 2014ApJ...796..131O, 2020ApJ...893...51S). p = 0.5 means Keplerian; p = 1 means
     conserved specific angular momentum (general practice).
   - Errors: Monte Carlo resampling within error bars (2015ApJ...812...27A; 1000 repeats in
     2017ApJ...834..178Y; 10000 resamplings in 2020ApJ...893...51S).
   - Alternative: the SLAM package's ridge and edge methods, then average the masses (2025ApJ...993..120H,
     2024A&A...690A..46S). 2025ApJ...993..120H rejected edge-method radii for the Keplerian radius and used
     ridge-method intersections of 12CO with 13CO/C18O profiles.
   - Correct the apparent break radius for resolution: 67 AU apparent became ~90 AU in 2015ApJ...812...27A;
     56 AU became ~74 AU in 2017ApJ...849...56A.
5. **Convert to mass and angular momentum** (general practice; assumptions in brackets).
   - Keplerian: M* = R V_rot^2 / G with V_rot = V_obs / sin i [point mass, disk mass neglected as in
     2019ApJ...873...73Z].
   - Centrifugal barrier: M = r_CB v_CB^2 / (2G) (2019ApJ...873...73Z). With conserved j, the centrifugal
     radius is r_c = l^2/GM and the barrier is r_0 = l^2/(2GM) (2017ApJ...843...27L).
   - Specific angular momentum j = R V_rot (deprojected). Example values: j = 6.1e-4 km/s pc in
     2014ApJ...796..131O (about 126 au km/s; 1 pc = 206265 au); 791 +/- 218 au km/s for the S CrA streamer
     in 2024A&A...683A.133G.
6. **Forward-model the envelope plus disk and compare with PV diagrams.** This is what most envelope papers
   do after step 4.
   - Ballistic infalling-rotating envelope (IRE, "CB model"): free M, r_CB, i; density r^-1.5; thin envelope
     (thickness 30 au in 2017ApJ...837..174O; 20 au in 2024ApJ...970...28O; h = 0.2 r in
     2019ApJ...873...73Z); beam convolution and a fixed intrinsic line width (0.5 km/s in 2015ApJ...812...59O,
     1 km/s in 2016ApJ...824...88O). FERIA implements IRE/Keplerian models and was fit by a reduced-chi2 grid
     in 2024ApJ...970...28O.
   - Infall slower than free fall: V_r = alpha V_ff with alpha ~0.3 (2015ApJ...812...27A,
     2017ApJ...849...56A); infall ratio A = 0.42 for a massive disk (2025A&A...693A..76C).
   - Free-fall plus conserved j, LTE line images, chi2 to PV with n0, T0, M*, j free and q fixed at -0.4
     (2017ApJ...834..178Y); same physics with chi2 over major and minor cuts (2019ApJ...871..100H).
   - Ulrich (rotating collapse) streamlines: centrifugal radius and initial polar angle as parameters
     (Rd = 700 au, theta0 = 70 deg, M = 1.1 Msun in 2020A&A...634L..12D; particle model with M* = 1.8 Msun,
     centrifugal radius 100 au and density r^-1.5 in 2019A&A...623A..96Y).
   - Full radiative transfer when temperature and abundance structure matter: RADMC-3D
     (2020ApJ...893...51S, 2021ApJ...908..108F, 2019ApJ...871..100H), LIME (2018A&A...614A.113H), MOLLIE and
     Hyperion (2015ApJ...813L..19J).
   - Run models through `simobserve` or the CASA simulator so they suffer the same spatial filtering
     (2015ApJ...812...27A, 2020ApJ...893...51S, 2014ApJ...796..131O).
7. **Streamers.** Fit point-mass trajectories (Mendoza et al. 2009 type) to the streamer in position and
   velocity. TIPSY grids LOS separation, plane-of-sky speed and direction and picks the trajectory that puts the
   largest fraction of points within their error bars, ties broken by chi2 (2024A&A...683A.133G).
   2025SciA...11.4512O used a streamline grid (rc 400-600 au in 50 au steps, theta0 75-85 deg) chosen by visual
   inspection with velocity_tools. Then infall time, energy and Mdot = M_streamer / T_inf
   (2024A&A...683A.133G).
8. **Infall from absorption.** Look for redshifted absorption against compact continuum. Confirm it is
   confined to the continuum source with off-source spectra (2015ApJ...814...22E). Infall velocity = velocity
   of the deepest absorption channel relative to V_LSR (5.1 km/s at 1.54 arcsec in 2018A&A...615A.141B). Fit
   absorption on cubes that are not continuum subtracted, with background, emission and foreground layers
   (2018A&A...615A.141B used XCLASS this way). Spherical collapse models with one free parameter (infall
   radius) and tested alternatives (2015ApJ...814...22E).
9. **Infall rate.** Flattened: Mdot = 2 pi R V_r Sigma, (1.5-3.0)e-6 Msun/yr at 100-200 AU in
   2015ApJ...812...27A. Spherical shell (general practice): Mdot = Omega r^2 rho v_in, with Omega the covered
   solid angle (2017A&A...603A..10B: r ~80 AU, rho ~2.3e9 cm-3, v_in ~7.1 km/s, disk covering ~50%). Streamer:
   M/T_inf (2024A&A...683A.133G) or v_ff M / l (2025SciA...11.4512O).

