### 2024A&A...683A.133G: TIPSY: Trajectory of Infalling Particles in Streamers around Young stars. Dynamical analysis of the streamers around S CrA and HL Tau (2024; star_formation, protoplanetary_disks; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Are observed streamer-like structures around S CrA and HL Tau consistent with infalling motion, and what are their dynamical parameters (energy, angular momentum, infall time, mass infall rate)?
- step 3: Fit infall trajectories
  - method: Grid over 3 free parameters (LOS separation, POS speed, POS direction) with Mendoza2009 trajectories; spline interpolation at distance metric; best fit = highest fraction within error bars, tie broken by lowest chi-squared [2.2 Fitting procedure: "We consider the best-fit trajectory as the one that can accommodate the highest fraction of mean values representing the observed streamer, within their error bars."]
  - software: TIPSY, scipy
  - assumption: Only point-mass gravity of the protostar; stellar mass, distance, systemic velocity fixed [2.4 Caveats: "we consider only one force acting on the infalling material: the gravitational force of a point mass (protostar)."]
  - assumption: Line intensity traces gas density [2.4 Caveats: "we also assume that the observed intensity of molecular-line emission represents the actual density distribution of gas."]
  - fitting: Grid search, no sampler; goodness = fitting fraction and chi-squared [Fitting procedure: "We consider the best-fit trajectory as the one that can accommodate the highest fraction of mean values representing the observed streamer, within their error bars. In cases where multiple trajectories fit the same fraction of values, we choose the trajectory with the lowest chi-squared deviation"]
  - validation: Implementation compared with two-body REBOUND simulations [2.1 Physical model: "were also validated through two-body simulations using the REBOUND framework"]
- step 5: Derive energies, angular momentum, infall time
  - method: Specific kinetic and potential energy, r0 x v0, infall time to closest approach from best-fit trajectory [4.2 Physical parameters: "specific (per unit mass) kinetic energy can be estimated as $0.5\times(v_{RA}^{2}+v_{Decl.}^{2}+v_{LOS}^{2})$."]
  - software: TIPSY
- step 6: Estimate streamer mass and infall rate
  - method: Optically thin lower-limit mass from integrated flux (Bergin2013 formula); Mdot = M_streamer / T_inf [4.2 Physical parameters: "estimate mass infall rates as $\dot{M}_{inf}=M_{streamer}/T_{inf}$"]
  - parameter: A=6.038e-7 s^-1, Eu=15.87 K (13CO); A=1.453e-3 s^-1, Eu=25.68 K (HCO+); Q=19.6, 12.0 at 25 K [4.2 Physical parameters: "were 19.6 and 12.0 for $^{13}$CO and HCO$^{+}$, respectively"]
  - assumption: T=25 K, abundances 1.45e-6 (13CO), 1e-9 (HCO+), optically thin [4.2 Physical parameters: "For both sources, we assumed a representative temperature of 25 K"]
  - validation: Treated as order-of-magnitude estimates [4.2 Physical parameters: "these values should be treated as an order of magnitude estimates"]
